ded 30 June 2026)", "period_end_date": "2026-06-30", "generated_at": "2026-07-27T08:09:48.571944+00:00", "note_text": "Keells Food Products posted Group revenue of LKR 2.11bn for Q2 2026 (quarter ended 30 June 2026), up 10% YoY, but Group PAT fell 27% YoY (LKR 18.7m to LKR 13.6m). QoQ, PAT collapsed 90.0% from Q1 2026 (Jan-Mar, LKR 135.96m) -- an even sharper version of the seasonal pattern seen at CCS, plausibly Sinhala/Tamil New Year demand concentrated in Q1, though this project cannot confirm that directly from the filing. The YoY gap between revenue growth and profit decline is a finance-cost story, not an operating one: operating profit (results from operating activities) grew a healthy 7% YoY, but net finance cost jumped 64% (finance cost +51%) as interest expense on borrowings rose sharply, eating into the operating gain and pulling PBT down 27% YoY. Tax expense fell 25% YoY in line with the lower PBT. Company-level and Group-level results are near-identical this quarter (KFP's subsidiary, John Keells Food India, is a small share of the business).", "source_doc_id": "52076", "source_page": 1, "gaps_note": "QoQ and YoY both now use actual filed interim reports for every period (Q1 2025, Q2 2025, Q1 2026, Q2 2026) -- one report per as-of-date, no re-extraction of a period from a second/audited filing. The seasonal explanation for the QoQ swing is this project's inference, not something the filing itself states.", "takeaway": "Like CCS, KFP's steep QoQ drop is most likely New Year seasonality working in reverse, not a genuine deterioration; the more meaningful signal is the YoY squeeze from rising finance costs.", "qoq": [{"metric": "revenue", "label": "Revenue", "prior_period_label": "Q1 2026 (quarter ended 31 March 2026)", "prior_value": 2294679.0, "new_period_label": "Q2 2026 (quarter ended 30 June 2026)", "new_value": 2112382.0, "pct_change": -0.07944335569375935, "direction": "deteriorated", "prior_period_end_date": "2026-03-31", "new_period_end_date": "20